# SAC-ALRA: next steps in exact order

This guide replaces the earlier version. The Compose stack intentionally has no
`ollama` service. Ollama already runs outside this project and uses these model
stores:

- `10.61.247.253`: `/data/.ollama`; SAC-ALRA web port `2018`
- `10.61.247.254`: `/data1/jai/.ollama`; SAC-ALRA web port `3000`

The examples assume both existing Ollama APIs listen on port `11434`. The ports
`2018` and `3000` are the SAC-ALRA browser ports, not Ollama model-directory
ports. If an Ollama API actually uses another port, change only
`OLLAMA_BASE_URL` in that server's `.env`.

Never run the connected bundle builder on either offline production server.

## Immediate recovery for the current proxy-contaminated container

This temporary recovery can restore Chroma access before the permanent bundle
is rebuilt. In the current production directory, keep its existing image tag
unchanged and add all six entries below to the `openwebui.environment` section
of its current `docker-compose.yml`:

```yaml
      HTTP_PROXY: ""
      HTTPS_PROXY: ""
      NO_PROXY: ""
      http_proxy: ""
      https_proxy: ""
      no_proxy: ""
```

Then recreate only OpenWebUI from the same image. A restart is insufficient
because container environment variables are fixed when the container is
created:

```bash
docker compose --env-file .env -f docker-compose.yml config --quiet
docker compose --env-file .env -f docker-compose.yml up -d \
  --no-build --force-recreate openwebui
docker compose --env-file .env -f docker-compose.yml exec -T openwebui sh -c '
  for variable in HTTP_PROXY HTTPS_PROXY NO_PROXY http_proxy https_proxy no_proxy; do
    value="$(printenv "${variable}" 2>/dev/null || true)"
    printf "%s=<%s>\n" "${variable}" "${value}"
  done'
```

Every value between `<` and `>` must be empty. Then run the current Chroma
diagnostic. This runtime override is safe even when the old image metadata still
contains the builder proxy.

The permanent correction below uses the new image tag
`sacai/openwebui:v0.11.3-sacalra1-amd64`. Do not retag an old image with that
name; generate and transfer the new archive from the connected builder.

## Part A — Mac

### 1. Create the corrected AlmaLinux source archive

On the Mac, from the repository root:

```bash
cd /Users/hiya_38/ISRO_SAC/sacai
chmod +x sacai-platform/scripts/*.sh
./sacai-platform/scripts/create_alma_source_bundle_macos.sh
```

The script excludes `.git`, `.github`, Git metadata, caches, and Mac files. It
creates:

```text
sacai-platform/dist/sacai-alma-builder-source.tar.gz
sacai-platform/dist/sacai-alma-builder-source.tar.gz.sha256
```

### 2. Copy both files to the connected AlmaLinux 9 AMD64 machine

Use the approved transfer method. Do not copy only individual edited files;
the archive and its checksum are one matched pair.

## Part B — connected AlmaLinux 9 AMD64 machine

### 3. Verify and extract the new source archive

```bash
mkdir -p "$HOME/sac-alra-builder"
cd "$HOME/sac-alra-builder"
sha256sum --check sacai-alma-builder-source.tar.gz.sha256
tar -xzf sacai-alma-builder-source.tar.gz
cd sacai-alma-builder
sha256sum --check SOURCE_MANIFEST.sha256
```

Stop if either checksum command reports a failure.

### 4. Confirm the builder platform and Docker

```bash
uname -m
cat /etc/os-release
docker version
docker compose version
docker info
```

The machine must be AlmaLinux 9.x and `uname -m` must print `x86_64`.

### 5. Confirm the connected-builder-only proxy

Only temporary build/download containers on the internet-connected AlmaLinux
machine use this proxy. The builder defaults to this value when
`SACAI_BUILD_PROXY_URL` is unset:

```text
http://http.docker.internal:3128
```

The build script passes this proxy to Node, Python, pip, apt, and model-cache
download operations. Docker image pulls are performed by Docker Desktop itself,
not by Dockerfile build arguments.

Seeing the proxy string in this guide or in
`scripts/prepare_connected_bundle.sh` is expected. Seeing a non-empty value for
`HTTP_PROXY`, `HTTPS_PROXY`, `http_proxy`, or `https_proxy` in the production
OpenWebUI container is a runtime proxy leak and must fail the deployment gate.
The final Docker stage and Compose environment clear all uppercase and lowercase
variants before OpenWebUI starts.

On this network, do not export an empty proxy value. If it was previously set
empty, restore the builder default:

```bash
unset SACAI_BUILD_PROXY_URL
```

In Docker Desktop, open **Settings → Proxies** and configure both the Docker
Desktop proxy and the Containers proxy using the organization-approved
system/manual proxy. Prefer **System proxy** and **Same as host proxy** when
those are the approved settings. Apply the change, restart Docker Desktop if it
requests that, and prove registry access before running the bundle builder:

```bash
docker pull --platform=linux/amd64 python:3.11-slim-bookworm
```

Do not continue until it succeeds. A `registry-1.docker.io` TLS handshake
timeout occurs before any Dockerfile instruction and cannot be repaired with a
project `--build-arg`.

To use a different container proxy:

```bash
export SACAI_BUILD_PROXY_URL=http://different-proxy.example:3128
```

Use a direct connection only on a different builder where direct HTTPS has
already been proven:

```bash
export SACAI_BUILD_PROXY_URL=
```

The builder next tests Alpine access from a temporary container. It does not
override `http.docker.internal` with `host-gateway`, because Docker Desktop owns
that internal DNS name. If the preflight fails, fix Docker Desktop proxy/DNS;
restarting the complete bundle build will not repair an unreachable proxy.

### 6. Build every Linux/AMD64 offline dependency

```bash
cd "$HOME/sac-alra-builder/sacai-alma-builder/sacai-platform"
chmod +x scripts/*.sh
./scripts/run_on_alma_builder.sh
```

The builder downloads every direct and transitive Python dependency as a
compatible Linux/AMD64 Python 3.11 wheel. The offline images install those
wheels with `pip install --no-index`, run `pip check`, and run the test suite.
It also stages `libexpat1` under `offline/apt-cache/rootfs/`. It does not pull,
copy, or package Ollama or its models.

### 7. Verify the generated offline bundle

```bash
test -s offline/requirements-linux-amd64.lock.txt
test -s offline/WHEELHOUSE_SHA256SUMS
test -s offline/apt-cache/PACKAGES.txt
test -e offline/apt-cache/rootfs/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libexpat.so.1
find offline/wheelhouse -maxdepth 1 -name '*.whl' | wc -l
./scripts/verify_offline_bundle.sh
```

The wheel count must be greater than zero and verification must pass.

### 8. Create and verify the production transfer archive

Replace the example destination with the mounted approved transfer medium:

```bash
./scripts/create_offline_transfer_archive.sh /mnt/encrypted-transfer
cd /mnt/encrypted-transfer
sha256sum --check sacai-rhel95-offline-amd64.tar.sha256
sync
```

Transfer the `.tar` and `.tar.sha256` files to both production servers.

## Part C — perform these common steps on both offline servers

Complete steps 9 through 17 first on `10.61.247.253`, then repeat them on
`10.61.247.254`.

### 9. Verify the host and the transfer archive

```bash
uname -m
cat /etc/redhat-release
docker version
docker compose version
cd /mnt/encrypted-transfer
sha256sum --check sacai-rhel95-offline-amd64.tar.sha256
```

Stop on a checksum failure. The production architecture must be `x86_64`.

### 10. Verify the already-running Ollama API before touching SAC-ALRA

On `10.61.247.253`:

```bash
test -d /data/.ollama
curl --fail --silent --show-error http://10.61.247.253:11434/api/tags
```

On `10.61.247.254`:

```bash
test -d /data1/jai/.ollama
curl --fail --silent --show-error http://10.61.247.254:11434/api/tags
```

The response must contain the expected model tags. If it fails, stop and fix
the existing Ollama listener or firewall first. SAC-ALRA does not mount these
directories and does not manage the Ollama process.

### 11. Extract the corrected bundle into a new local directory

Do not overwrite the directory that failed at old step 21. Keeping it intact
makes rollback possible.

```bash
mkdir -p "$HOME/sac-alra-production-v2"
tar -xf /mnt/encrypted-transfer/sacai-rhel95-offline-amd64.tar \
  -C "$HOME/sac-alra-production-v2"
cd "$HOME/sac-alra-production-v2/sacai-alma-builder/sacai-platform"
./scripts/verify_offline_bundle.sh
```

### 12. Create the correct host-specific `.env`

On `10.61.247.253`:

```bash
cp .env.server-253.example .env
```

On `10.61.247.254`:

```bash
cp .env.server-254.example .env
```

Generate two different secrets on each server:

```bash
openssl rand -hex 32
openssl rand -hex 32
vi .env
```

Put the first value in `WEBUI_SECRET_KEY` and the second in
`SACAI_INTERNAL_TOKEN`. Confirm these host-specific values:

```text
10.61.247.253: PROJECT_NAME=SAC-ALRA, SACAI_WEB_PORT=2018,
                OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://10.61.247.253:11434
10.61.247.254: PROJECT_NAME=SAC-ALRA, SACAI_WEB_PORT=3000,
                OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://10.61.247.254:11434
```

If every OpenWebUI instance should list models from both Ollama servers,
uncomment this optional line in both `.env` files:

```dotenv
OLLAMA_BASE_URLS=http://10.61.247.253:11434;http://10.61.247.254:11434
```

If a vLLM instance is already running on this host with an OpenAI-compatible
API (e.g. `10.61.247.254:8004`), set these three lines in that host's `.env`
so OpenWebUI connects to it alongside Ollama, not instead of it. The URL must
include the `/v1` suffix; `OLLAMA_BASE_URL` never does, but `OPENAI_API_BASE_URLS`
always does:

```dotenv
ENABLE_OPENAI_API=true
OPENAI_API_BASE_URLS=http://10.61.247.254:8004/v1
OPENAI_API_KEYS=EMPTY
```

`EMPTY` is vLLM's own placeholder key for a server started without
`--api-key`; use the real key instead if this vLLM deployment enforces one.
Leave `ENABLE_OPENAI_API=false` (the default) on any server with no vLLM
instance, since an empty `OPENAI_API_BASE_URLS` otherwise falls back to the
public OpenAI API, which an offline host cannot reach.

### 13. Load images, prepare output paths, and validate Compose without internet

```bash
./scripts/load_offline_images.sh
./scripts/prepare_output_host_paths.sh
docker compose --env-file .env -f docker-compose.yml config --quiet
```

There should be no Ollama image and no compose `ollama` service. That is
intentional.

`prepare_output_host_paths.sh` creates the `SACAI_OUTPUT_HOST_PATH` /
`SACAI_AUDIT_HOST_PATH` / `SACAI_TOOL_VERSIONS_HOST_PATH` directories from
`.env` (default `./offline/data/{outputs,audit,tool_versions}`) and confirms
they are writable. These are plain host directories, not Docker named
volumes, so scientific outputs, the audit trail, and installed tool versions
stay directly browsable/backupable outside any container. If this host
previously ran a release that used the old `sacai-outputs`/`sacai-audit`/
`sacai-tool-versions` named volumes, run
`./scripts/migrate_named_volumes_to_bind_mounts.sh` first so that data is not
left behind.

This release also moves the vendored OpenWebUI source from `v0.10.2` to
`v0.11.3`. If this host already has an `openwebui-data` volume from an
earlier SAC-ALRA deployment (real chats, users, or settings, not a fresh
volume), back it up before continuing — the rebuild below runs OpenWebUI's
own Alembic migrations against it in one step, and that is not reversible:

```bash
./scripts/backup_openwebui_data.sh
```

It no-ops with a message if no `openwebui-data` volume exists yet (a
genuinely first-time install), otherwise it copies the volume to a timestamped
directory under `offline/data/`.

### 14. Rebuild and start SAC-ALRA offline

Without ISIS3:

```bash
./scripts/rebuild_offline.sh
```

With a completely pre-staged ISIS3 image and data:

```bash
COMPOSE_PROFILES=isis ./scripts/rebuild_offline.sh
```

The OpenWebUI build has an 8 GiB Node heap. The Docker VM or host still needs
enough physical memory for that build.

The rebuild is deliberately `--no-cache`, force-recreates the container, checks
that its immutable image ID matches the newly built `sacalra1` tag, and fails if
any uppercase or lowercase proxy variable has a non-empty runtime value. Do not
use `docker history` timestamps as the acceptance test; use the image-ID and
environment checks printed by `rebuild_offline.sh`.

### 15. Check Chroma first, then OpenWebUI

```bash
docker compose --env-file .env -f docker-compose.yml ps
docker compose --env-file .env -f docker-compose.yml logs \
  --tail=100 chroma openwebui sacai-api planetir-worker notebook-worker redis
./scripts/diagnose_chroma.sh
```

Do not add `ollama` to the compose log command; it is not a compose service.
The corrected Chroma container stores data at `/data`, exposes the v2 heartbeat,
and must become `healthy` before OpenWebUI starts. OpenWebUI has a two-minute
startup grace period for its offline caches and database migrations.

If `diagnose_chroma.sh` still fails, save and send its complete output. Do not
delete the `chroma-data` volume: it may contain indexed user data and the logs
must identify the actual failure before any recovery action.

### 16. Confirm the external Ollama connection from the container

This check is already included at the end of `diagnose_chroma.sh`. It must print
the configured `/api/tags` URL and HTTP status `200`.

If this OpenWebUI data volume was previously started with
`http://ollama:11434`, its database may retain that old admin setting. Once the
UI opens, go to **Admin Settings → Connections → Ollama**, replace the old URL
with the same external URL from `.env`, and save. Do not reset the whole
OpenWebUI database to change one connection.

If `ENABLE_OPENAI_API=true` was set, confirm vLLM the same way from inside the
container, using the same URL as `OPENAI_API_BASE_URLS`:

```bash
docker compose --env-file .env -f docker-compose.yml exec -T openwebui \
  curl --fail --silent --show-error -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' \
  http://10.61.247.254:8004/v1/models
```

It must print `200`. Then in **Admin Settings → Connections**, confirm an
OpenAI connection now exists at that URL and that its model list is populated;
if it is not, the vLLM server is not actually reachable from inside the
container (check the server is bound to `0.0.0.0`, not `127.0.0.1`, and that
no firewall separates the Docker network from port `8004`).

### 17. Open the correct SAC-ALRA web address

For server `10.61.247.253`:

```text
http://10.61.247.253:2018
```

For server `10.61.247.254`:

```text
http://10.61.247.254:3000
```

Each SAC-ALRA deployment has its own OpenWebUI, Chroma, Redis, job, and audit
data. Only Ollama model serving is external to the stack.

After installing the `sacalra1` frontend, open a private browser window or clear
the site's cached application data so an older JavaScript bundle is not reused.
Confirm both corrected frontend paths:

```bash
curl --fail --silent --show-error \
  http://localhost:"$(sed -n 's/^SACAI_WEB_PORT=//p' .env)"/static/sacai-logo.svg \
  | head -1
```

The response must begin with `<svg`. Then create and save a trivial Tool while
the browser Network panel is open. The format request and the subsequent
`POST /api/v1/tools/create` must both succeed. A `localeCompare` error at
`compareVersion` means the browser or container is still serving an older
frontend image.

## Part D — finish application setup after both deployments are healthy

### 18. Create the first administrator on each instance

Use the approved organization account policy. Do not expose Redis, Chroma, or
the internal SAC-ALRA API to employee networks.

### 19. Install the Tools

In **Workspace → Tools → Create Tool**, install:

1. `openwebui_tools/stac_catalog.py`
2. `openwebui_tools/planetir.py`
3. `openwebui_tools/scientific_workflow.py`
4. `openwebui_tools/isis3_preprocess.py` only when ISIS is enabled
5. `openwebui_tools/admin_cleanup.py` only for an admin-restricted model
6. `openwebui_tools/jupyter_runtime.py` only when the external Jupyter runtime is approved

If `stac_catalog` already exists, edit that same Tool record and replace its
complete source with the new `openwebui_tools/stac_catalog.py`; do not create a
second STAC Tool. Open its Valves, inspect the live schema, and confirm the
`sun incidence` alias maps to the real catalog field. The supplied default is
`view:incidence_angle`.

For service-backed Tools, configure:

```text
service_url = http://sacai-api:8000
service_token = the exact SACAI_INTERNAL_TOKEN from this server's .env
artifact_root = /data/outputs
```

For `jupyter_runtime`, also set `jupyter_server_url`, its dedicated token,
`kernel_name`, and an exact `allowed_notebooks` JSON mapping. Start with one
known notebook. Users provide only its friendly code name and a `product_id`.
See `stage-14/README.md`; do not place the Jupyter address or token in `.env` or
the Tool source.

### 20. Install the grounding Filter and prompts

Create a Function from `openwebui_functions/grounding_guard.py`, configure its
service URL and token as above, and enable it on scientific models. Copy the
appropriate complete prompt into each Workspace model:

- `prompts/planetir_system_prompt.md`
- `prompts/coder_system_prompt.md`

### 21. Run acceptance tests

In order: list STAC collections; inspect the TMC collection/schema; request sun
incidence values for two known product IDs; request them for `2020 TMC`; verify
that missing values, unknown IDs, and any result truncation are explicit; test
an unsupported STAC field; upload the reference GeoTIFF; submit and poll
PlanetIR; download its JSON/PNG/GeoTIFF outputs; test user isolation; and verify
the numeric grounding warning. If ISIS is enabled, test one raw mission product
and one already-calibrated GeoTIFF.

When Jupyter integration is enabled, also run Tool actions `list` and `check`,
submit one allowlisted notebook/product ID, poll it to completion, download its
executed notebook/result, and verify cooperative cancellation with a slow test
notebook.

### 22. Record the accepted deployment

Append a dated entry to `BUILD_LOG.md` with host address, web port, external
Ollama URL and model tags, image IDs, bundle checksums, Docker/RHEL versions,
test results, and `PROJECT_NAME=SAC-ALRA`. Keep the original transfer archive
read-only through the rollback window.
